= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Set $t=2^x>0$. The <exponential function> identity $4^x=(2^x)^2$ reduces the problem to the <quadratic equation>
$$
t^2-t-2=(t-2)(t+1)=0.
$$
Positivity excludes $t=-1$, so $2^x=2$. Since the <exponential function> $2^x$ is strictly increasing, the unique answer is $\boxed{x=1}$.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Join the centre of the <unit circle> to adjacent vertices of the <regular polygon>. Each of the $n$ resulting <triangles> has two sides of <length> $1$ and included <angle> $2\pi/n$. Bisecting one gives a <right triangle>, so each polygon side, a <chord of a circle>, has <length> $2\sin(\pi/n)$. Thus the perimeter is $2n\sin(\pi/n)$.

Each of these <triangles> has <area> $\tfrac12\sin(2\pi/n)$, so the polygon's <area> is $\tfrac n2\sin(2\pi/n)$. Comparing the two expressions gives
$$
\boxed{m=2,\qquad k=2.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Vector addition>[Adding the vectors] component by component gives
$$
\mathbf u+\mathbf v=(5a,0,2a)^T=\mathbf0.
$$
Consequently $\boxed{a=0}$. The requirement that the <vectors> be nonzero then implies $b\ne0$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Since $\mathbf v=-\mathbf u$ and $\mathbf u\ne\mathbf0$, the <vectors> point in opposite directions. More explicitly, their <dot product> gives
$$
\cos\theta=\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}=-1.
$$
Hence their <angle> is $\boxed{\theta=\pi\text{ radians}=180^\circ}$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <derivative> is
$$
f'(x)=\frac{2x}{a^2}-\frac{2a^2}{x^3}.
$$
A <stationary point> at $x=1$ requires $2/a^2-2a^2=0$, or $a^4=1$. Since $a>0$, $\boxed{a=1}$.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the requested parameter value $a=2$, the <function graph> has two branches, symmetric about the vertical axis because $f$ is an <even function>. Its <derivatives> are
$$
f'(x)=\frac x2-\frac8{x^3}=\frac{x^4-16}{2x^3},\qquad f''(x)=\frac12+\frac{24}{x^4}>0.
$$
The <stationary points> are $\boxed{(-2,2)\text{ and }(2,2)}$, both <local minima> and also the lowest points of their respective branches. The <function> tends to $+\infty$ as $x\to0^\pm$, so $x=0$ is a vertical <asymptote>; it also tends to $+\infty$ as $|x|\to\infty$. The negative branch decreases to $(-2,2)$ and then rises towards the vertical <asymptote>; the positive branch falls from the vertical <asymptote> to $(2,2)$ and then rises.

\Image[paper-1-q4-graph.png]
{title=Two branches and minima of the function in Question 4 for $a=2$}

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
