= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The double-angle identity for the <cosine> gives $\cos^2x-1=(\cos2x-1)/2$. Termwise <integration> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\int(\cos^2x-1)\,dx=\frac14\sin2x-\frac x2+C.}
$$
Here $C$ is an arbitrary constant in the <antiderivative>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <derivative> of the <tangent> is $\sec^2x$, and the order of the endpoints must be retained. Thus the <definite integral> is
$$
\left[\tan x\right]_{\pi/3}^{\pi/6}=\frac1{\sqrt3}-\sqrt3=\boxed{-\frac2{\sqrt3}}.
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Group consecutive terms in pairs. The $j$th pair, for $j=1,\ldots,25$, is
$$
-\frac{4j-3}{2}+\frac{4j-1}{2}=1.
$$
There are $25$ such pairs in this finite <sum>, so the answer is $\boxed{25}$.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

This <geometric series> has first term $1/27$ and common ratio $1/3$, whose <absolute value> is below one. Consequently its <sum> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{1/27}{1-1/3}=\frac1{18}}.
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Writing $v=y-1$ turns the <first-order linear differential equation> into $v'=v$. Its general solution is $v=Ce^x$, so $y=1+Ce^x$. The <initial condition> gives $C=-1$, hence
$$
y(x)=1-e^x,\qquad \boxed{y(1)=1-e}.
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The general solution of the <first-order linear differential equation> is $y=1+Ce^x$. The <initial condition> $y(0)=1$ gives $C=0$, so the solution of this <initial value problem> is the constant <function> $y(x)=1$. Thus $\boxed{y(1)=1}$.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

For the <partial fraction decomposition>, multiply by $(x-1)(x-2)$ to obtain the <polynomial identity>
$$
x^2+1=a(x-1)(x-2)+b(x-2)+c(x-1).
$$
Comparing the $x^2$ coefficients gives $a=1$. Substitution of $x=1$ gives $2=-b$, while $x=2$ gives $5=c$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a=1,\qquad b=-2,\qquad c=5.}
$$
The original identity holds for $x\ne1,2$; evaluating the multiplied <polynomial identity> at those values is valid.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
