= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Subtracting the two <circle> equations cancels the common terms and gives $-4x=0$. With $x=0$, either <circle> equation gives $(y-1)^2=0$. Thus the unique common <point> is
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(0,1)}.
$$
The two <circles> touch externally there.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <circles> have centres $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1)$. Their <Euclidean distance> is
$$
\boxed{\sqrt{(1-(-1))^2+(1-1)^2}=2}.
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The two throws are independent, giving $36$ equally likely ordered outcomes in the <sample space>. A total strictly below $4$ occurs only for $(1,1)$, $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$. Hence the <probability> is
$$
\boxed{\frac3{36}=\frac1{12}}.
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The favourable outcomes are $(j,j+1)$ and $(j+1,j)$ for $j=1,\ldots,5$. These are ten distinct ordered outcomes among the $36$ equally likely elements of the <sample space>, so the <probability> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{10}{36}=\frac5{18}}.
$$

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

This is a <plane> through the origin in three-dimensional <Euclidean space>, with nonzero <normal vector> $(1,2,-3)^T$:
$$
\boxed{\{(x,y,z):x+2y-3z=0\}\text{ is a plane through }\mathbf0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For example, choose
$$
\boxed{\mathbf u=(-2,1,0)^T,\qquad \mathbf v=(3,0,1)^T.}
$$
Both <vectors> satisfy the <plane equation>. They are <linearly independent vectors>: in $\alpha\mathbf u+\beta\mathbf v=\mathbf0$, the second coordinate gives $\alpha=0$ and the third gives $\beta=0$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No.** Every <vector> in the <plane> can be written as
$$
(x,y,z)^T=(-2y+3z,y,z)^T=y(-2,1,0)^T+z(3,0,1)^T.
$$
The two <linearly independent vectors> in part (ii) therefore form a <vector space basis> for this <vector subspace>. Its <dimension of a vector space>[dimension] is $2$, so any three <vectors> in it have <linear dependence>.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <dot product> with the given <unit vector> is $\mathbf r\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}=(x+z)/\sqrt2$. Subtracting the corresponding <orthogonal projection> gives
$$
\mathbf r-\hat{\mathbf n}(\mathbf r\cdot\hat{\mathbf n})=\left(\frac{x-z}{2},y,\frac{z-x}{2}\right)^T.
$$
Taking its squared <Euclidean norm> yields the equivalent <Cartesian coordinates>[Cartesian] equation
$$
\boxed{\frac{(x-z)^2}{2}+y^2=4}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The removed <vector> is the <orthogonal projection> of $\mathbf r$ onto the <line> through the origin in direction $\hat{\mathbf n}$. The remaining <vector> measures the perpendicular displacement from that <line>. Fixing its <Euclidean norm> at $2$ therefore describes **an infinite circular <cylinder> of radius $2$**, with axis $x=z$, $y=0$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <plane equation> $x+z=0$ has <normal vector> $(1,0,1)^T=\sqrt2\hat{\mathbf n}$. Thus its <normal vector> is parallel to the <cylinder>'s axis and the <plane> is perpendicular to that axis. It passes through the origin, so the intersection is **a <circle> of radius $2$ centred at the origin in that <plane>**.

Indeed, with the <orthonormal basis> $\mathbf e=(1,0,-1)^T/\sqrt2$ and $\mathbf f=(0,1,0)^T$ of the <plane>, write $\mathbf r=s\mathbf e+t\mathbf f$. The <cylinder> equation becomes $s^2+t^2=4$. Equivalently, the intersection has the <parametric equation>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r(\theta)=2\cos\theta\,\mathbf e+2\sin\theta\,\mathbf f,\quad0\leq\theta<2\pi.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Use the <distance from a point to a plane> formula with the <unit normal> $\hat{\mathbf n}$. Since the <plane> passes through the origin, the distance is
$$
\boxed{d=|\mathbf p\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}|=\frac{|2+0|}{\sqrt2}=\sqrt2}.
$$
For a direct check, the <closest point on a plane> is $\mathbf p-(\mathbf p\cdot\hat{\mathbf n})\hat{\mathbf n}=(1,\sqrt2,-1)^T$, whose displacement from $\mathbf p$ has <Euclidean norm> $\sqrt2$.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $\mathbf d=\mathbf a_2-\mathbf a_1$ and $\mathbf w=\mathbf v_1\times\mathbf v_2$. The assumed <linear independence> gives $\mathbf w\ne\mathbf0$. Every displacement joining one <line> to the other is
$$
\mathbf s=\mathbf d+\lambda_2\mathbf v_2-\lambda_1\mathbf v_1.
$$
Its <dot product> with $\mathbf w$ is always $\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf w$. The component in that direction is therefore fixed, so <orthogonal decomposition> gives $\|\mathbf s\|\geq|\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf w|/\|\mathbf w\|$.

The two <linearly independent vectors> $\mathbf v_1,\mathbf v_2$ span the <plane> perpendicular to $\mathbf w$. Suitable $\lambda_1,\lambda_2$ cancel the component of $\mathbf d$ in that <plane>, attaining the lower bound. Thus the <distance between skew lines> formula, also valid when the <lines> intersect, gives
$$
\boxed{d(L_1,L_2)=\frac{|(\mathbf a_2-\mathbf a_1)\cdot(\mathbf v_1\times\mathbf v_2)|}{\|\mathbf v_1\times\mathbf v_2\|}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The nonzero direction <vectors> now have <linear dependence>, so the <lines> are parallel or coincident. Subtract from $\mathbf d=\mathbf a_2-\mathbf a_1$ its <orthogonal projection> along $\mathbf v_1$; the remaining component is the shortest joining displacement. The <distance between parallel lines> is
$$
\boxed{d(L_1,L_2)=\left\|\mathbf d-\frac{\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf v_1}{\mathbf v_1\cdot\mathbf v_1}\mathbf v_1\right\|=\frac{\|\mathbf d\times\mathbf v_1\|}{\|\mathbf v_1\|}.}
$$
The second expression follows from the relation between the <cross product> and the perpendicular component. It includes distance zero for coincident <lines>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the stipulated <linearly independent vectors> $\mathbf v_1,\mathbf v_2$, the denominator in part (i) is nonzero. Thus
$$
\boxed{d(L_1,L_2)=0\iff(\mathbf a_2-\mathbf a_1)\cdot(\mathbf v_1\times\mathbf v_2)=0.}
$$
Equivalently, $\mathbf a_2-\mathbf a_1$ belongs to the <linear span> of $\mathbf v_1,\mathbf v_2$. Geometrically the <lines> lie in one <plane>, and, since their direction <vectors> are not parallel, **they intersect at a unique <point>**. The <linear combination> expressing $\mathbf a_2-\mathbf a_1$ gives the two parameters of that intersection.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <exponential series> to obtain the <Maclaurin series> of the <hyperbolic sine> and <hyperbolic cosine>:
$$
\sinh x=x+\frac{x^3}{6}+O(x^5),\qquad \cosh x=1+\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^4).
$$
Dividing these <power series> gives the <hyperbolic tangent> expansion
$$
\tanh x=\left(x+\frac{x^3}{6}+O(x^5)\right)\left(1-\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^4)\right)=\boxed{x-\frac{x^3}{3}+O(x^5)}.
$$
The displayed remainder uses <Big O notation> as $x\to0$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $\ell=\ln2$. Applying the <exponential series> and the <Maclaurin series> of the <sine> gives
$$
2^x-2^{-x}=e^{\ell x}-e^{-\ell x}=2\ell x+\frac{\ell^3}{3}x^3+O(x^5),\qquad \sin x=x-\frac{x^3}{6}+O(x^5).
$$
Multiply the two <power series>, retaining every contribution through degree four:
$$
(2^x-2^{-x})\sin x=\boxed{2(\ln2)x^2+\frac{(\ln2)^3-\ln2}{3}x^4+O(x^6)}.
$$
Since $0<\ln2<1$, the coefficient of $x^4$ is nonzero, so these are indeed the first two nonzero terms. The remainder is in <Big O notation>.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $F(x)=\ln(e^{ax}-b)$. Direct <differentiation>, using the <chain rule>, gives
$$
F'(x)=\frac{ae^{ax}}{e^{ax}-b},\qquad F''(x)=-\frac{a^2be^{ax}}{(e^{ax}-b)^2}.
$$
The stated parameter restrictions ensure that the <logarithm> is defined near $x=1$, and that $F'(1)>0$ and $F''(1)<0$. With $h=x-1$, the <Taylor series> begins
$$
F(1+h)=\ln(e^a-b)+\frac{ae^a}{e^a-b}h-\frac{a^2be^a}{2(e^a-b)^2}h^2+O(h^3).
$$
If $e^a-b\ne1$, the constant term is nonzero, and the first two nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{\ln(e^a-b)+\frac{ae^a}{e^a-b}(x-1)+O((x-1)^2).}
$$
This covers both $a>\ln(1+b)$ and $0<a<\ln(1+b)$; the constant term is respectively positive or negative.

If $a=\ln(1+b)$, which is possible precisely with $0<a<\ln2$ and $b=e^a-1$, the constant term vanishes. The first two nonzero terms are then
$$
\boxed{a(1+b)(x-1)-\frac{a^2b(1+b)}2(x-1)^2+O((x-1)^3).}
$$
No further case is needed, since the linear and quadratic coefficients cannot vanish under the given restrictions. All remainders use <Big O notation>.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply <integration by parts> twice. Taking an <antiderivative> of $e^{x/2}$ to be $2e^{x/2}$ gives
$$
\int x^2e^{x/2}\,dx=2x^2e^{x/2}-4\int xe^{x/2}\,dx,
$$
and the second application gives $\int xe^{x/2}\,dx=2xe^{x/2}-4e^{x/2}+C$. Therefore the required <definite integral> is
$$
\left[2e^{x/2}(x^2-4x+8)\right]_0^1=\boxed{10\sqrt e-16}.
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The factor $x^2$ is an <even function> and $\sin x$ is an <odd function>, so their product is an <odd function>. Its <definite integral> over the symmetric <interval> vanishes:
$$
\boxed{0}.
$$
Indeed the <substitution rule> $x=-t$ changes the integral over $[-\pi/2,0]$ into the negative of the integral over $[0,\pi/2]$.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <partial fraction decomposition> gives
$$
\frac1{x(x^2+1)}=\frac1x-\frac{x}{x^2+1}.
$$
For the second term the <substitution rule> $t=x^2+1$, $dt=2x\,dx$ gives a <logarithm>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{dx}{x(x^2+1)}=\ln|x|-\frac12\ln(x^2+1)+C,\qquad x\ne0.}
$$
The constant in the <antiderivative> may be chosen independently on the two <intervals> separated by $0$.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Split the <integral> into
$$
\int\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}+\int\frac{x\,dx}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}.
$$
The first is the standard <antiderivative> $\operatorname{arsinh}x=\ln(x+\sqrt{1+x^2})$ from the <inverse hyperbolic functions>. For the second, the <substitution rule> $t=1+x^2$ gives $\tfrac12\int t^{-1/2}\,dt=\sqrt{1+x^2}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\ln(x+\sqrt{1+x^2})+\sqrt{1+x^2}+C.}
$$
The <logarithm>'s argument is positive for every real $x$.

= e
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Use the <tangent half-angle substitution> $t=\tan(x/2)$, so $\cos x=(1-t^2)/(1+t^2)$ and $dx=2\,dt/(1+t^2)$. Then
$$
\int\frac{dx}{5+3\cos x}=\int\frac{2\,dt}{5(1+t^2)+3(1-t^2)}=\int\frac{dt}{t^2+4}.
$$
The <inverse tangent> <antiderivative> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac12\arctan\left(\frac12\tan\frac x2\right)+C.}
$$
This expression applies on each <interval> $( (2j-1)\pi,(2j+1)\pi)$ for an integer $j$. Although $5+3\cos x\geq2$ everywhere, the chosen <inverse tangent> expression jumps at the endpoints because the substitution has a pole there. A global continuous <antiderivative> is obtained by adding $j\pi/2$ on that <interval>, using one common $C$, and extending by <continuity> at the endpoints.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <probability density function> must have total <integral> one. Put $h=\pi/6$. The term $ax$ is an <odd function>, so
$$
1=\int_{-h}^{h}(ax+b\cos x)\,dx=2b\sin h=b.
$$
Thus $\boxed{b=1}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With $b=1$, nonnegativity of the <probability density function> at both $t$ and $-t$ requires
$$
\cos t\geq |a|t,\qquad 0<t\leq h=\pi/6.
$$
This is equivalent to $|a|\leq\inf_{0<t\leq h}\cos t/t$. The <derivative>
$$
\frac d{dt}\left(\frac{\cos t}{t}\right)=-\frac{t\sin t+\cos t}{t^2}<0
$$
shows that this infimum occurs at $t=h$. Therefore the necessary and sufficient restriction is
$$
\boxed{-\frac{3\sqrt3}{\pi}\leq a\leq\frac{3\sqrt3}{\pi}.}
$$
Sufficiency follows from the same decreasing-ratio argument at every interior <point>. The endpoint values of $a$ are allowed: the <probability density function> merely vanishes at one endpoint.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $h=\pi/6$, the <expected value> is
$$
\mathbb E[X]=\int_{-h}^h x(ax+\cos x)\,dx=a\int_{-h}^h x^2\,dx=\frac{2ah^3}{3},
$$
because $x\cos x$ is an <odd function>. Comparing with the specified <expected value> gives $\boxed{a=1}$, which lies in the permitted parameter range.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Here $a=0$, so the <probability density function> is the <even function> $\cos x$ on $[-h,h]$, where $h=\pi/6$. Hence $\mathbb E[X]=0$ by <odd function>[oddness], and the <variance> is just $\mathbb E[X^2]$. Two applications of <integration by parts> give
$$
\int x^2\cos x\,dx=x^2\sin x+2x\cos x-2\sin x+C.
$$
Thus
$$
\operatorname{Var}(X)=2\left[h^2\sin h+2h\cos h-2\sin h\right]=\boxed{\frac{\pi^2}{36}+\frac{\pi\sqrt3}{3}-2}.
$$

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Keep $a$ general initially, since the final part does not explicitly fix it. For $-h\leq y\leq h$, where $h=\pi/6$, the <cumulative distribution function> is
$$
F_X(y)=\int_{-h}^y(ax+\cos x)\,dx=\frac a2(y^2-h^2)+\sin y+\frac12.
$$
The <normalization constant> in the <probability density function> of $Y$ must be
$$
M=\int_{-h}^h F_X(y)\,dy=\frac a2\left(\frac{2h^3}{3}-2h^3\right)+h=\boxed{h-\frac{2ah^3}{3}=\frac\pi6-\frac{a\pi^3}{324}.}
$$
Equivalently, the <integrated cumulative distribution function on a bounded interval> identity gives $M=h-\mathbb E[X]$. If the assumption $a=0$ from part (c) is retained, the requested special case is $\boxed{M=\pi/6}$. For the value $a=1$ in part (b), it is $M=\pi/6-\pi^3/324$.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Where $\cos x\ne0$, the <separable differential equation> is $y'=y\tan x$. Separating and taking <antiderivatives> gives $\ln|y|=-\ln|\cos x|+C$, for nonzero $y$. Including the zero solution, the explicit general solution is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C\sec x,\qquad C\in\mathbb R,}
$$
on any <interval> with $\cos x\ne0$. It can also be obtained without dividing by $y$: the <product rule> gives $(y\cos x)'=0$.

A nonzero solution cannot extend through a zero of $\cos x$. If a differentiable solution is required on an <interval> containing such a zero, the original equation there requires $y=0$, and the only such solution across that zero is $y\equiv0$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $x\ne0$ and $y\ne0$, put $v=y/x$. This is the standard substitution for a <homogeneous first-order differential equation>. Since $y'=v+xv'$, the equation becomes
$$
1+v^2-v(v+xv')=0,\qquad vv'=\frac1x.
$$
Taking <antiderivatives> yields $v^2=2\ln|x|+C$. The explicit general solution is therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\pm x\sqrt{2\ln|x|+C},}
$$
on any <interval> where $x\ne0$ and $2\ln|x|+C>0$, with a fixed choice of sign. For a check that does not divide by $y$, set $w=y^2$ to get $w'-2w/x=2x$ and hence $w=x^2(2\ln|x|+C)$.

There is no missing zero solution: at $y=0$ with $x\ne0$, the original equation would require $x^2=0$. Also no real solution on an <interval> about $x=0$ can arise from this family, since $2\ln|x|+C$ becomes negative near $0$.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the given <differential form>, set $P=x(y^4+1)$ and $Q=y(2x^2y^2+1)$. Their <partial derivatives> satisfy
$$
P_y=4xy^3=Q_x.
$$
In fact an explicit potential is
$$
F(x,y)=\frac12x^2(y^4+1)+\frac12y^2,
$$
since $F_x=P$ and $F_y=Q$. Thus the <differential form> is an <exact differential form> on all of $\mathbb R^2$.

Replacing $y^2$ by $u$ suggests the potential
$$
H(x,u)=\frac12\left[x^2(u^2+1)+u\right].
$$
Direct <differentiation> verifies that $dH/dx$ is exactly the left-hand side of the required <ordinary differential equation>. This direct verification remains valid even for negative $u$; it does not require a real square root $y=\sqrt u$. Therefore
$$
x^2u^2+u+x^2=C.
$$
The <initial condition> gives $C=1$. Solving this <quadratic equation> in $u$ and selecting the branch that has $u(1)=0$ gives
$$
\boxed{u(x)=\frac{-1+\sqrt{1+4x^2-4x^4}}{2x^2}=\frac{2(1-x^2)}{1+\sqrt{1+4x^2-4x^4}}.}
$$
The second expression also defines the removable value $u(0)=1$. The maximal open <interval> containing $1$ on which this is a differentiable real solution is
$$
\boxed{-\sqrt{\frac{1+\sqrt2}{2}}<x<\sqrt{\frac{1+\sqrt2}{2}}.}
$$
At either endpoint the square root vanishes and $2x^2u+1=0$, while $x(u^2+1)\ne0$, so the original <ordinary differential equation> cannot hold there with a finite <derivative>.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Translate the coordinates by $u=x-1$ and $v=y+1$, and write $E=e^{-(u^2+v^2)/2}>0$. The <partial derivatives> are
$$
f_x=v(1-u^2)E,\qquad f_y=u(1-v^2)E.
$$
If $u=0$ or $v=0$, these equations together force $u=v=0$. Otherwise they require $u^2=v^2=1$. Thus the complete set of <stationary points> is
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(1,-1),\ (2,0),\ (0,-2),\ (2,-2),\ (0,0).}
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At the central <stationary point>, $u=v=0$, so $f=0$. At each of the other <stationary points>, $u^2+v^2=2$ and hence $f=uv/e$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
f(1,-1)&=0,\\
f(2,0)=f(0,-2)&=e^{-1},\\
f(2,-2)=f(0,0)&=-e^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
$$

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the translated coordinates $u=x-1$, $v=y+1$, the zero <level set> is the pair of <lines> $u=0$ and $v=0$. The <function> is positive in the quadrants $uv>0$ and negative in those with $uv<0$, and tends to zero as $u^2+v^2\to\infty$. Thus its nonzero <contour plot>[contours] form closed curves around the four off-centre <stationary points>.

For an explicit description of the <contour plot>, use <polar coordinates> $u=r\cos\theta$, $v=r\sin\theta$:
$$
f=\frac{r^2}{2}e^{-r^2/2}\sin2\theta.
$$
At fixed direction within a quadrant, the magnitude increases until $r=\sqrt2$ and then decreases towards zero. Also $|u|e^{-u^2/2}\leq e^{-1/2}$, with equality exactly when $|u|=1$, and similarly for $v$. Consequently $|f|\leq e^{-1}$; each positive level below $e^{-1}$ encloses a positive peak in each positive quadrant, with the analogous negative contours in the negative quadrants.

To classify the <stationary points>, the <Hessian matrix> is
$$
\nabla^2f=E\begin{pmatrix}uv(u^2-3)&(1-u^2)(1-v^2)\\(1-u^2)(1-v^2)&uv(v^2-3)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $u=v=0$ it is $\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$, with <eigenvalues> $1$ and $-1$, so $(1,-1)$ is a <saddle point>. At $u,v\in\{-1,1\}$ it is $-2fI$. Therefore **$(2,0)$ and $(0,-2)$ are strict <local maxima>, and $(2,-2)$ and $(0,0)$ are strict <local minima>**. These are also global maxima and minima by the bound just proved.

\Image[paper-1-q16-contours.png]
{title=Contours and gradient directions near all five stationary points in Question 16}

= d
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <gradient> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=E\bigl(v(1-u^2),u(1-v^2)\bigr)^T.}
$$
It is perpendicular to each nonzero <level set> and points towards increasing $f$. Near the two positive <local maxima>, the <Hessian matrix> is $-2e^{-1}I$, so the <gradient> arrows point inward. Near the two negative <local minima>, the <Hessian matrix> is $2e^{-1}I$, so they point outward. These arrows are shown on the figure in part (c).

At the <saddle point>, $\nabla f\approx(v,u)^T$. Along $v=u$, the arrows point away from the centre on both sides; along $v=-u$, they point towards the centre on both sides. On the positive $u$ axis they point up, on the negative $u$ axis down, on the positive $v$ axis right, and on the negative $v$ axis left. This specifies the four intervening sectors of the <gradient> pattern.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

By the definition of <matrix multiplication> and <trace>, with all indices summed from $1$ to $D$,
$$
\operatorname{tr}(PQR)=\sum_{i,j,k}P_{ij}Q_{jk}R_{ki}.
$$
The entries are scalars, so their multiplication commutes. Reordering them and renaming the summed indices gives
$$
\sum_{i,j,k}Q_{jk}R_{ki}P_{ij}=\sum_{j,k,i}Q_{jk}R_{ki}P_{ij}=\operatorname{tr}(QRP).
$$
Equivalently, in <Einstein notation>, $P_{ij}Q_{jk}R_{ki}=Q_{jk}R_{ki}P_{ij}$. This proves the <cyclic property of the trace>.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <outer product> of the column <vector> with its <matrix transpose> is the $2\times2$ <matrix>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf n\mathbf n^T=\begin{pmatrix}n_1^2&n_1n_2\\n_1n_2&n_2^2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The reversed product is the <dot product>, a scalar represented by a $1\times1$ <matrix>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf n^T\mathbf n=n_1^2+n_2^2.}
$$

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply <matrix multiplication> to obtain
$$
M\mathbf x=\mathbf x-\hat{\mathbf n}(\hat{\mathbf n}^T\mathbf x)=\mathbf x-(\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\mathbf x)\hat{\mathbf n}.
$$
Since $\hat{\mathbf n}$ is a <unit vector>, the subtracted term is the <orthogonal projection> of $\mathbf x$ along $\hat{\mathbf n}$. Hence **$M$ is the <orthogonal projection onto a plane> through the origin with <normal vector> $\hat{\mathbf n}$**. It fixes every <vector> perpendicular to $\hat{\mathbf n}$ and sends every <scalar multiple> of $\hat{\mathbf n}$ to zero. In particular $\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot M\mathbf x=0$.

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Using <Einstein notation> and the <Kronecker delta>,
$$
\operatorname{tr}M=M_{ii}=\delta_{ii}-\hat n_i\hat n_i=3-\|\hat{\mathbf n}\|^2=\boxed{2}.
$$
The <unit vector> condition is essential in the last step.

= e
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <matrix> is
$$
P=I-\mathbf p\mathbf p^T=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&0\\-1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},
$$
so
$$
\boxed{P\mathbf x=(-x_2,-x_1,x_3)^T.}
$$
Here $\|\mathbf p\|^2=2$, so $P=I-2\hat{\mathbf p}\hat{\mathbf p}^T$ with $\hat{\mathbf p}=\mathbf p/\sqrt2$. This is a <Householder transformation>: **reflection in the <plane> $x_1+x_2=0$**. The component parallel to the <normal vector> $\mathbf p$ changes sign, while the perpendicular component is fixed.

= f
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The second <matrix> acts by
$$
Q=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad Q\mathbf x=(x_2,x_1,x_3)^T.
$$
Applying $P$ from part (e) then gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y=PQ\mathbf x=(-x_1,-x_2,x_3)^T,\qquad PQ=\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1).}
$$
The <Householder transformation> $Q$ reflects in the <plane> $x_1-x_2=0$. Its <normal vector> $\mathbf q$ is perpendicular to $\mathbf p$, so the two reflecting <planes> meet at a right angle along the $x_3$ axis. Their composition is **a rotation through $\pi$ about the $x_3$ axis**, as is also immediate from the displayed <rotation matrix>.

= g
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

From part (f), $PQ=\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1)$. To obtain $PQR=-I$, choose the <Householder transformation> that reverses only the third coordinate:
$$
\boxed{R=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-1),\qquad \mathbf r=(0,0,\sqrt2)^T.}
$$
Indeed $\mathbf r\mathbf r^T=\operatorname{diag}(0,0,2)$, so $R_{ij}=\delta_{ij}-r_ir_j$, and direct <matrix multiplication> gives $PQR=-I$. The alternative <vector> $-\mathbf r$ produces the same <matrix>. Geometrically $R$ reflects in the <plane> $x_3=0$, completing the reversal of all three coordinates.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $I_n$ denote the <integral> with the <cosine> factor and $J_n$ the one with the <sine> factor. In <integration by parts>, take $u=\cos(nx)$ and $dv=e^x\,dx$. Then
$$
I_n=[e^x\cos(nx)]_0^\pi+nJ_n=\boxed{(-1)^ne^\pi-1+nJ_n}.
$$
The integer restriction gives $\cos(n\pi)=(-1)^n$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $J_n$, take $u=\sin(nx)$ and $dv=e^x\,dx$ in <integration by parts>. This yields
$$
J_n=[e^x\sin(nx)]_0^\pi-nI_n=\boxed{-nI_n},
$$
since $\sin(n\pi)=\sin0=0$ for every integer $n$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $D_n=(-1)^ne^\pi-1$. The two <linear equations> from parts (i) and (ii) give $I_n=D_n+nJ_n$ and $J_n=-nI_n$, so $(1+n^2)I_n=D_n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{I_n=\frac{(-1)^ne^\pi-1}{1+n^2},\qquad J_n=\frac{n\left[1-(-1)^ne^\pi\right]}{1+n^2}.}
$$
These formulas include $n=0$, when $I_0=e^\pi-1$ and $J_0=0$.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <Fourier series> represents a periodic <function> as a constant plus <sine> and <cosine> waves whose frequencies are integer multiples of its fundamental frequency. For period $2L$, use the convention
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left[a_n\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)+b_n\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)\right],
$$
where the <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
a_0&=\frac1L\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\,dx,\\
a_n&=\frac1L\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)\,dx,\quad n\geq1,\\
b_n&=\frac1L\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)\,dx,\quad n\geq1.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The coefficient formulas follow by multiplying by a basis <sine> or <cosine> and using their <orthogonality> over a full period. A <Fourier series> need not equal an arbitrary <function> at every <point>. The <pointwise convergence of a piecewise smooth Fourier series> theorem says that for a piecewise continuously differentiable <function>, the <Fourier series> converges at each <point> to the average of the two one-sided limits, and hence to $f(x)$ wherever $f$ is continuous.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <function> is even, so each product with a <sine> is an <odd function> and all the <Fourier coefficients> $b_n$ vanish. Its constant coefficient is
$$
a_0=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi e^x\,dx=\frac{2(e^\pi-1)}\pi.
$$
For $n\geq1$, part (a) gives the remaining <Fourier coefficients>:
$$
a_n=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi e^x\cos(nx)\,dx=\frac{2\left[(-1)^ne^\pi-1\right]}{\pi(1+n^2)}.
$$
Consequently the <Fourier cosine series> is
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac{e^\pi-1}{\pi}+\frac2\pi\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^ne^\pi-1}{1+n^2}\cos(nx).}
$$
The periodic extension is continuous, including at $\pm\pi$, and piecewise continuously differentiable. Thus the <Fourier series> equals $f$ everywhere. Its coefficients are also bounded in magnitude by a constant times $1/n^2$, which gives <absolute convergence>.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At $x=\pi/2$, all odd-indexed <cosine> terms vanish, while $\cos(2k\pi/2)=(-1)^k$. Thus the <Fourier series> from part (c) gives
$$
e^{\pi/2}=\frac{e^\pi-1}{\pi}+\frac{2(e^\pi-1)}\pi\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^k}{4k^2+1}.
$$
Divide by $2(e^\pi-1)/\pi$ and rearrange. The <hyperbolic sine> identity $e^\pi-1=2e^{\pi/2}\sinh(\pi/2)$ yields
$$
\boxed{\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^k}{4k^2+1}=\frac{\pi}{4\sinh(\pi/2)}-\frac12,\qquad m=4.}
$$
The evaluation is legitimate by the convergence established in part (c).

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

All $a_n$ are positive, and $a_1=e^{-1}$. The <logarithm> turns consecutive ratios into differences, producing the finite <telescoping sum>
$$
\sum_{k=1}^{n}u_k=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\bigl(\ln a_{k+1}-\ln a_k\bigr)=\ln a_{n+1}+1.
$$
Thus, already for every finite $n$,
$$
a_{n+1}=\exp\left(-1+\sum_{k=1}^{n}u_k\right).
$$
If the <partial sums> converge to $S$, the <continuity> of the <exponential function> gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}a_n=\exp\left(-1+\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}u_k\right)=e^{-1+S}.}
$$
The shift from $a_{n+1}$ to $a_n$ preserves the <limit of a sequence>. Convergence of the <partial sums> is established in part (f), so no convergence assumption is left unsupported.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Cancellation of the <factorials> in the consecutive ratio gives
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=e^{-1}\left(1+\frac1n\right)^{n+1/2},\qquad u_n=\left(n+\frac12\right)\ln\left(1+\frac1n\right)-1.
$$
Set $t=1/n$. The <derivative> of the <natural logarithm> at $1$ gives $\ln(1+t)/t\to1$. Therefore
$$
u_n=\left(1+\frac t2\right)\frac{\ln(1+t)}t-1\longrightarrow\boxed{0}.
$$
Thus the necessary condition from the <term test for divergence> is satisfied. On its own it does not prove that $\sum u_n$ converges.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $n>1$, the <Taylor series> of the <natural logarithm> converges absolutely at $1/n$:
$$
\ln\left(1+\frac1n\right)=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}{jn^j}.
$$
Multiply this <power series> by $n+1/2$ and subtract $1$. The constant term cancels the subtracted $1$, and the coefficient of $1/n$ is $-1/2+1/2=0$. For $k\geq2$, the coefficient of $n^{-k}$ is
$$
\frac{(-1)^k}{k+1}+\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{2k}=(-1)^k\left(\frac1{k+1}-\frac1{2k}\right).
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{u_n=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^k}{n^k}\left(\frac1{k+1}-\frac1{2k}\right).}
$$
In particular $u_n=1/(12n^2)-1/(12n^3)+O(n^{-4})$, in <Big O notation>.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

A <rational lower bound for the natural logarithm> proves positivity for every $n$, including $n=1$. Define
$$
h(t)=\ln(1+t)-\frac{2t}{2+t},\qquad t\geq0.
$$
Then $h(0)=0$, while direct <differentiation> gives
$$
h'(t)=\frac1{1+t}-\frac4{(2+t)^2}=\frac{t^2}{(1+t)(2+t)^2}>0\quad(t>0).
$$
Thus $h(t)>0$ for $t>0$. Setting $t=1/n$ in the expression from part (b) gives
$$
u_n=\frac{2+t}{2t}\left[\ln(1+t)-\frac{2t}{2+t}\right]=\frac{2+t}{2t}h(t)>0.
$$
Therefore $\boxed{u_n>0\text{ for every }n\geq1}$, and $(a_n)$ is a strictly increasing <sequence>.

= e
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For $k\geq2$, write
$$
c_k=\frac1{k+1}-\frac1{2k}=\frac{k-1}{2k(k+1)},\qquad 0<c_k<\frac12.
$$
Use positivity from part (d) and bound the absolute terms of the <power series> in part (c) by a <geometric series>. For $n\geq2$,
$$
0<u_n\leq\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\frac{c_k}{n^k}<\frac12\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\frac1{n^k}=\frac1{2n^2(1-1/n)}\leq\frac1{n^2}.
$$
For $n=1$, $u_1=\tfrac32\ln2-1<\tfrac12<1$, since $\ln2=\int_1^2dt/t<1$. Thus a bound valid for every positive integer $n$ is
$$
\boxed{0<u_n<An^m,\qquad A=1,\quad m=-2.}
$$

= f
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

By part (e) and the <comparison test for series>, $\sum u_n$ converges, since the <P-series> $\sum n^{-2}$ converges. Put $S=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}u_n$. Part (a) now proves
$$
\boxed{a_n\longrightarrow e^{-1+S}\in(0,\infty).}
$$
Part (d) also shows that $(a_n)$ increases towards this finite positive <limit>. This proves <convergence of the Stirling sequence> without presupposing its limiting constant.

For identification of that constant, the independently known <Stirling's formula> $n!\sim\sqrt{2\pi n}(n/e)^n$ gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}a_n=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}.}
$$
The comparison argument above supplies the convergence proof requested by the preceding parts; the <Stirling's formula> identification is an additional evaluation.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $G=x^2/a^2+y^2/a^2+z^2/b^2-1$. Its <gradient> cannot vanish on the <ellipsoid>, since the origin is not on the surface. Thus the <Lagrange multiplier> equations capture every constrained <stationary point>:
$$
\boxed{4x^3=\frac{2\lambda x}{a^2},\qquad 2yz^2=\frac{2\lambda y}{a^2},\qquad 2y^2z=\frac{2\lambda z}{b^2},\qquad \frac{x^2+y^2}{a^2}+\frac{z^2}{b^2}=1.}
$$
The <temperature> is a <sum> of squares, so $T\geq0$. Equality requires $x=0$ and $yz=0$. The surface constraint then gives the four coldest <points>:
$$
\boxed{T_{\min}=0,\qquad (x,y,z)=(0,\pm a,0)\text{ or }(0,0,\pm b).}
$$
These are all such <points>, because a <sum> of nonnegative terms can vanish only when each term vanishes.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Solve the <Lagrange multiplier> equations by separating cases according to which coordinates vanish, so that division by a coordinate loses no <stationary points>.

If $x\ne0$ and $y=z=0$, the surface constraint gives
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(\pm a,0,0),\qquad T=a^4.}
$$
If $x\ne0$ and exactly one of $y,z$ is nonzero, the equation for that nonzero coordinate forces $\lambda=0$, while the $x$ equation gives $\lambda=2a^2x^2>0$. Hence there are no <stationary points> in that case.

If $x=0$ and $y,z\ne0$, the two remaining equations give $\lambda=a^2z^2=b^2y^2$. The surface constraint therefore has equal contributions from $y$ and $z$, giving four <stationary points>:
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(0,\varepsilon\frac a{\sqrt2},\eta\frac b{\sqrt2}\right),\qquad T=\frac{a^2b^2}{4},\quad\varepsilon,\eta\in\{-1,1\}.}
$$
If all three coordinates are nonzero, the equations imply
$$
\lambda=2a^2x^2=a^2z^2=b^2y^2,
$$
so $z^2=2x^2$ and $y^2=2a^2x^2/b^2$. Substitution in the surface constraint gives, with $D=4a^2+b^2$,
$$
x^2=\frac{a^2b^2}{D},\qquad y^2=\frac{2a^4}{D},\qquad z^2=\frac{2a^2b^2}{D}.
$$
Thus the remaining eight <stationary points> and their <temperatures> are
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\varepsilon_1\frac{ab}{\sqrt D},\varepsilon_2\frac{\sqrt2a^2}{\sqrt D},\varepsilon_3\frac{\sqrt2ab}{\sqrt D}\right),\qquad T=\frac{a^4b^2}{4a^2+b^2},\quad\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2,\varepsilon_3\in\{-1,1\}.}
$$
For example, their common <temperature> follows from $x^4+y^2z^2=a^4b^4/D^2+4a^6b^2/D^2=a^4b^2/D$. The cases with $x=0$ and $yz=0$ are exactly the coldest <points> already listed. This exhausts all possibilities, giving fourteen other <stationary points> in addition to those four.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $a=b=1$, the <stationary points> found in parts (a) and (b) have <temperatures> $0$, $1$, $1/4$ and $1/5$. The <unit sphere> is compact and $T$ is a <continuous function>, so the <extreme value theorem> ensures that a hottest <point> exists; it must be one of those <stationary points>. Thus
$$
\boxed{T_{\max}=1,\qquad (x,y,z)=(1,0,0)\text{ or }(-1,0,0).}
$$
For a direct bound, put $s=x^2\in[0,1]$. The identity $(y^2-z^2)^2\geq0$ gives $y^2z^2\leq(y^2+z^2)^2/4=(1-s)^2/4$, and
$$
T\leq s^2+\frac{(1-s)^2}{4}\leq1,
$$
since $1-s^2-(1-s)^2/4=(1-s)(5s+3)/4\geq0$. Equality forces $s=1$, giving exactly the two <points> above.
